  "transcript": [
    {
      "t": 0.0,
      "text": "What I want to do in this video",
      "speaker": "S1"
    },
    {
      "t": 1.56,
      "text": "is familiarize ourselves with a very common class",
      "speaker": "S1"
    },
    {
      "t": 5.64,
      "text": "of sequences.",
      "speaker": "S1"
    },
    {
      "t": 6.86,
      "text": "This is arithmetic sequences, and they're usually pretty",
      "speaker": "S1"
    },
    {
      "t": 12.7,
      "text": "easy to spot.",
      "speaker": "S1"
    },
    {
      "t": 13.92,
      "text": "They're sequences where each term is a fixed",
      "speaker": "S1"
    },
    {
      "t": 16.3,
      "text": "number larger than the term before it.",
      "speaker": "S1"
    },
    {
      "t": 19.0,
      "text": "My goal here is to figure out which",
      "speaker": "S1"
    },
    {
      "t": 20.82,
      "text": "of these sequences are arithmetic sequences, and then",
      "speaker": "S1"
    },
    {
      "t": 23.58,
      "text": "just so that we have some practice with",
      "speaker": "S1"
    },
    {
      "t": 25.08,
      "text": "some of the sequence notation, I want to",
      "speaker": "S1"
    },
    {
      "t": 26.92,
      "text": "define them in either as explicit functions of",
      "speaker": "S1"
    },
    {
      "t": 30.26,
      "text": "the term you're looking for, the index you're",
      "speaker": "S1"
    },
    {
      "t": 32.1,
      "text": "looking at, or as recursive definitions.",
      "speaker": "S1"
    },
    {
      "t": 35.14,
      "text": "So first, given that an arithmetic sequence is",
      "speaker": "S1"
    },
    {
      "t": 37.5,
      "text": "one where each successive term is a fixed",
      "speaker": "S1"
    },
    {
      "t": 39.72,
      "text": "amount larger than the previous one, which of",
      "speaker": "S1"
    },
    {
      "t": 42.2,
      "text": "these are arithmetic sequences?",
      "speaker": "S1"
    },
    {
      "t": 45.84,
      "text": "Let's look at this first one right over",
      "speaker": "S1"
    },
    {
      "t": 47.56,
      "text": "here.",
      "speaker": "S1"
    },
    {
      "t": 47.86,
      "text": "To go from negative five to negative three,",
      "speaker": "S1"
    },
    {
      "t": 50.36,
      "text": "we had to add two.",
      "speaker": "S1"
    },
    {
      "t": 52.2,
      "text": "Then to go from negative three to negative",
      "speaker": "S1"
    },
    {
      "t": 54.02,
      "text": "one, you have to add two.",
      "speaker": "S1"
    },
    {
      "t": 56.2,
      "text": "Then to go from negative one to one,",
      "speaker": "S1"
    },
    {
      "t": 58.32,
      "text": "you had to add two.",
      "speaker": "S1"
    },
    {
      "t": 60.38,
      "text": "So this is clearly an arithmetic sequence.",
      "speaker": "S1"
    },
    {
      "t": 62.7,
      "text": "We're adding the same amount every time, and",
      "speaker": "S1"
    },
    {
      "t": 64.92,
      "text": "there's several ways that we can define the",
      "speaker": "S1"
    },
    {
      "t": 66.68,
      "text": "sequence.",
      "speaker": "S1"
    },
    {
      "t": 67.12,
      "text": "We could say it's a sub n, and",
      "speaker": "S1"
    },
    {
      "t": 70.36,
      "text": "you don't always have to use k.",
      "speaker": "S1"
    },
    {
      "t": 71.44,
      "text": "This time I'll use n to denote our",
      "speaker": "S1"
    },
    {
      "t": 73.18,
      "text": "index from n equals one to infinity with,",
      "speaker": "S1"
    },
    {
      "t": 78.48,
      "text": "and there's two ways we can define it.",
      "speaker": "S1"
    },
    {
      "t": 80.02,
      "text": "We can either define it explicitly or we",
      "speaker": "S1"
    },
    {
      "t": 81.64,
      "text": "can define it recursively.",
      "speaker": "S1"
    },
    {
      "t": 82.76,
      "text": "So if we wanted to define it explicitly,",
      "speaker": "S1"
    },
    {
      "t": 84.86,
      "text": "we could write a sub n is equal",
      "speaker": "S1"
    },
    {
      "t": 87.34,
      "text": "to whatever the first term is.",
      "speaker": "S1"
    },
    {
      "t": 88.92,
      "text": "In this case, our first term is negative",
      "speaker": "S1"
    },
    {
      "t": 91.78,
      "text": "five.",
      "speaker": "S1"
    },
    {
      "t": 92.92,
      "text": "It's equal to negative five, plus we're going",
      "speaker": "S1"
    },
    {
      "t": 96.36,
      "text": "to add two, one less times than the",
      "speaker": "S1"
    },
    {
      "t": 99.72,
      "text": "term we're at.",
      "speaker": "S1"
    },
    {
      "t": 100.48,
      "text": "So for the second term, we add two",
      "speaker": "S1"
    },
    {
      "t": 101.78,
      "text": "once.",
      "speaker": "S1"
    },
    {
      "t": 102.28,
      "text": "For the third term, we add two twice.",
      "speaker": "S1"
    },
    {
      "t": 104.5,
      "text": "For the fourth term, we added from our",
      "speaker": "S1"
    },
    {
      "t": 106.88,
      "text": "base term, we added two three times.",
      "speaker": "S1"
    },
    {
      "t": 108.6,
      "text": "So we're going to add two, we're going",
      "speaker": "S1"
    },
    {
      "t": 111.28,
      "text": "to add positive two, one less than the",
      "speaker": "S1"
    },
    {
      "t": 114.38,
      "text": "index that we're looking at, n minus one",
      "speaker": "S1"
    },
    {
      "t": 117.52,
      "text": "times.",
      "speaker": "S1"
    },
    {
      "t": 118.12,
      "text": "So this is an explicit definition of this",
